{"name":"Atlantis Myth","hex":"#006477","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"myth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A traditional story which embodies a belief regarding some fact or phenomenon of experience, and in which often the forces of nature and of the soul are personified; a sacred narrative regarding a god, a hero, the origin of the world or of a people, etc.","Such stories as a genre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/myth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atlantis","blocks":[{"text":"Atlantis is a fictional island mentioned in Plato's works Timaeus and Critias as part of an allegory on the hubris of nations. By describing Atlantis as a naval empire from the west that had conquered most of Europe and Libya, Plato purposely created a literary contrast with the Achaemenid Empire, the great land-based power that ruled the east. In Plato's account, Atlantis loses divine favor after an ill-fated campaign against a fictionalized Athens, and subsequently submerges into the Atlantic Ocean. By portraying the victorious Athens in the image of his ideal state from the Republic, Plato intended the Atlantis story to bear witness to the superiority of his concept of a state."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Atlantis_Kircher_Mundus_subterraneus_1678.jpg/330px-Atlantis_Kircher_Mundus_subterraneus_1678.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantis"},{"title":"Myth","blocks":[{"text":"Myth is a genre of folklore consisting primarily of narratives that play a fundamental role in a society. For scholars, this is different from the ordinary sense of the term myth, meaning a belief that is not true, as the veracity of a piece of folklore is entirely irrelevant to determining whether it constitutes a myth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Hotherus_and_wood_maidens_by_Froelich.jpg/330px-Hotherus_and_wood_maidens_by_Froelich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myth"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Platon.png/120px-Platon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Platon.png/250px-Platon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Platon.png/120px-Platon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlaton.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Plato_Timaeus.jpg/500px-Plato_Timaeus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Plato_Timaeus.jpg/500px-Plato_Timaeus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A fifteenth-century Latin translation of Plato's Timaeus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlato_Timaeus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg/500px-Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg/1280px-Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg/500px-Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Santorini caldera on 24 June 2022, taken from the International Space Station. From the Minoan eruption event, and the 1964 discovery of Akrotiri on the island, this location is one of many sites purported to have been the location of Atlantis.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Atlantic_Ocean_surface.jpg/500px-Atlantic_Ocean_surface.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Atlantic_Ocean_surface.jpg/500px-Atlantic_Ocean_surface.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The mid-atlantic ridge in the Atlantic","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtlantic_Ocean_surface.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Doggerland3er_en.png/500px-Doggerland3er_en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Doggerland3er_en.png/1280px-Doggerland3er_en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Doggerland3er_en.png/500px-Doggerland3er_en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A map showing the hypothetical extent of Doggerland from now back to the Weichselian glaciation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADoggerland3er_en.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/POxy1084_Hellanicus_Atlantis.png/500px-POxy1084_Hellanicus_Atlantis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/POxy1084_Hellanicus_Atlantis.png/500px-POxy1084_Hellanicus_Atlantis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A fragment of Atlantis by Hellanicus of Lesbos","link":"https://en.wiki